[[:db/cas 17592186092723 :question/retired nil true] [:db/cas 17592186092725 :question/retired nil true] [:db/cas 17592186234307 :question/retired nil true] [:db/cas 17592186317210 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{4},2\\right):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{3}}}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{′}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $f(x)=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{4},2\\right):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{3}}}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{′}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]]
